chunk_findings.detail:
The change replaces the MCP body encoding `json.dumps(..., indent=2)` with `json.dumps(..., separators=(",", ":"))`, and this new encoding is applied unconditionally to *every* `as_text=False` tool — not just the five projected reads. The CLI's `--json` serialization is not changed (the new comment itself states "MCP encodes it compact, the CLI indented"), so for any tool whose body is *not* a projection (i.e., has no `BOUNDS` entry, so `bound is None`), the MCP wire body and the CLI `--json` body now differ in whitespace. The original code documented byte-identity between these two as "the point" of sharing `Outcome.body`; this diff only redefines that invariant in a comment ("the two are the same parsed body"), which cannot change what an existing test asserts. Concretely, an unbounded tool: `_api().foo(...)` → `out.body(payload)` = `{"a":1,"b":2}`; CLI `--json` emits `{
  "a": 1,
  "b": 2
}` while MCP now emits `{"a":1,"b":2}`, so any parity test/consumer doing `assert mcp_text == cli_json_text` (which the pre-change comment says was the pinned property) fails. If the intent really is "CLI --json stays byte-identical to the MCP body wherever tests pin parity," the compact encoding must be applied only where the body is deliberately a projection (or the CLI must be compacted in lockstep).
